To force a text field to display columns in Adobe JavaScript, you can use the following approach:

1.  Create a text field in your Adobe application, such as a text input field in a form.
2.  Use the `textFormat` property to set the text format of the text field to a multi-line format, such as `textFormat: "richtext"`.
3.  Use the `columns` property to specify the number of columns you want the text to be displayed in. For example, `columns: 3`.
4.  You can also use the `columnWidth` property to specify the width of each column.

Here is an example of how you might implement this in JavaScript:

```javascript
// Get a reference to the text field
var textfield = this.getField("myTextField");

// Set the text format to a multi-line format
textfield.textFormat = "richtext";

// Set the number of columns
textfield.columns = 3;

// Set the width of each column
textfield.columnWidth = 100;
```

Note that the `columns` and `columnWidth` properties are only available in multi-line text fields, such as those with a `textFormat` of "richtext" or "multiline".

Alternatively, you can use the `columnCount` and `columnWidth` properties to achieve the same result:

```javascript
// Get a reference to the text field
var textfield = this.getField("myTextField");

// Set the text format to a multi-line format
textfield.textFormat = "richtext";

// Set the number of columns
textfield.columnCount = 3;

// Set the width of each column
textfield.columnWidth = 100;
```

Note that the `columnCount` property is only available in multi-line text fields, such as those with a `textFormat` of "richtext" or "multiline".

Also, you can use the `columnGap` property to specify the gap between columns.

```javascript
// Get a reference to the text field
var textfield = this.getField("myTextField");

// Set the text format to a multi-line format
textfield.textFormat = "richtext";

// Set the number of columns
textfield.columnCount = 3;

// Set the width of each column
textfield.columnWidth = 100;

// Set the gap between columns
textfield.columnGap = 10;
```

Please note that the above code is for Adobe Acrobat JavaScript, and it may not work in other Adobe applications.

